 splice, and set a wick by touch, keeping a lantern burning steadily with little oil.","category":"physical"},{"name":"Pattern memory","description":"Remembers the order of lamps and notices when a familiar sequence of lit nights has been altered.","category":"mental"}]},{"id":"chr_owen_rusk","name":"Owen Rusk","role":"antagonist","home_location_id":"loc_lantern_wall","faction_id":"fac_lamp_wardens","description":"Owen Rusk is the senior Lamp Warden, responsible for assigning the wall watches and teaching new keepers the rite. He has served since boyhood and believes the parish survived because wardens acted before councils could argue. Tall and stooped from years of leaning into niches against the wind, he wears a patched wool mantle and carries a long iron hook for tending lamps from the wall's safe side. He will not discuss the page cut from last winter's ledger, though he knows more about that season than he admits.","motivation":"To preserve the wardens' authority and keep the parish from repeating the catastrophe remembered as the Dark Winter.","goal":"Maintain the wall's lights and keep the missing-night question from reopening old blame before the parish can meet its oil obligations.","flaw":"He mistakes secrecy for protection and treats questions as threats to the order he serves.","internal_conflict":"Owen fears that a truthful account of last winter will reveal that his own choices failed the very duty he has built his life around.","external_conflict":"Mara's investigation challenges his control of the watch, while the Moot Council demands the ledger and the turf-cutters contest the wardens' oil count.","arc":"Owen begins convinced that silence is the only way to hold the wardens together. When Mara's watch reveals that the oil shortfall cannot be managed by his private tallies, he must choose between stopping her inquiry and letting the disputed account reach the council. He ends by acknowledging his part in the ledger's silence and allowing the record 